 ### Combined Depth Cueing and Shading (Reversed Order)
-A technique to combine depth cueing and directional shading by reversing the order of operations (shading first, then depth cueing) to use a single composite filter $H'$ and avoid two convolutions [S3]. It is chosen when computational efficiency is prioritized and the light direction is not perpendicular to the view direction, requiring an adjustment term [S3]. The adjustment term is $f(x) \nabla d(\mathbf{V} \cdot \mathbf{x}) \cdot \mathbf{L}$, which is zero if $d(x)$ is linear and $\mathbf{L}$ is perpendicular to $\mathbf{V}$ [S3].
+A technique to combine depth cueing and directional shading by reversing the order of operations (applying depth cueing and interpolation first, then shading) to use a single composite filter $H'$ and avoid two convolutions [S3]. It is chosen when computational efficiency is prioritized and the light direction is not perpendicular to the view direction, requiring an adjustment term [S3]. The adjustment term is $f(x) d'_{1D}(\mathbf{V} \cdot \mathbf{x}) (\mathbf{V} \cdot \mathbf{L})$, which is zero if $d(x)$ is linear and $\mathbf{L}$ is perpendicular to $\mathbf{V}$ [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** $O(n^2 \log n)$ for the frequency domain computation, replacing $O(n^3)$ spatial domain computations [S1]. The interpolation step is $O(n^2)$ and consumes most of the running time [S1].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the 3D spectrum and intermediate slices [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** A $128^3$ volume can be rendered in a fraction of a second on a conventional workstation [S1]. The adaptive filtering scheme reduced the cost of interpolation to about 15% of the non-relative case [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** A $128^3$ volume can be rendered in a fraction of a second on a conventional workstation [S1]. The adaptive filtering scheme reduced the cost of interpolation to about 15% of the non-adaptive case [S1].
 *   **Strengths:** Significant speed advantage over conventional volume rendering; no visible difference compared to conventional spatial domain rendering with identical shading and depth cueing [S1].
 *   **Limitations:** The frequency domain projection operator is non-occluding, resulting in a loss of realism [S1]. The reversed order requires an adjustment term if $\mathbf{L}$ is not perpendicular to $\mathbf{V}$ [S3].
-*   **When to Use:** When real-time or near-real-time rendering is required for large volumes and shading/depth cueing are needed [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Use when high performance is needed for large volumes and occlusion is not required [S1].
 *   **When Not to Use:** When occlusion is critical for realism or when the light direction is complex and non-perpendicular, requiring expensive convolutions for adjustment [S1, S3].
 
 ## Background
-Originated by Takashi Totsuka and Marc Levoy in 1993 [S1, S3]. The paper "Frequency Domain Volume Rendering" was presented at SIGGRAPH 1993 [S1]. The method was developed to reduce the computational cost of volume rendering by leveraging the Fourier Projection-Slice Theorem [S1]. It is used in medical imaging and scientific visualization where large volumes need to be rendered efficiently [S1].
+Originated by Takashi Totsuka and Marc Levoy in 1993 [S1, S3]. The paper "Frequency Domain Volume Rendering" was presented at SIGGRAPH 1993 [S1]. The method replaces $O(n^3)$ spatial domain computations (specifically shading and depth cueing) with $O(n^2)$ frequency domain methods to reduce computational cost [S1].
 
